          <p>Poland's eastern regions contain some of the darkest skies in the European Union. The Bieszczady Mountains in southeastern Poland — with their treeless połoniny highland meadows at Bortle Class 2 — rival the Scottish Highlands and far exceed anything available in Western Europe. The Tatra Mountains provide high-altitude astronomy with cable-car access to 2,000 metres, while Białowieża Primeval Forest offers the rare combination of ancient cathedral oaks framing the Milky Way. In northeastern Poland, the flat Masurian Lake District and Biebrza marshlands provide additional exceptional dark-sky environments, all within reach of Warsaw by car.</p>
